  "note": "The En's salient projects toward us (an enclave for us, MW s9 para 2 a p 124). Its approaches are few, obvious and strongly held, so frontal attacks astride them are very costly: take the tactically important features from the flanks or rear (para 4 a p 127) and pull his reserves forward ahead of its base (para 4 b). Laid on MW Figure 4 (p 131): the En fixed from the front while his base is attacked by envelopment combined with infiltration (para 5 a p 128); if the shoulders cannot be assailed, penetrate a shoulder with infiltration instead (para 5 b). The Left Bn makes the main attack onto the base at the L shoulder from the left flank, the Right Bn the secondary attack onto the R shoulder, the third Bn less two Coys fixes the arch, its A Coy with the Bde's commando Pls infiltrates to the base to block his reinforcement and withdrawal (para 10 p 132), its C Coy the Bde reserve on the main axis (a central reserve is of little value, MW s7 para 9 p 82); each assaulting Bn keeps its own reserve Coy. The force assembles at more than one place (para 8 b, d p 130). Deception on a broad front with the opening artillery weight away from the points of attack (para 12 pp 132-133); the artillery split across the prongs and the DAA in two (paras 13-14 p 133). Each prong is a Bn attack (no brigaded FUP: the prongs are wide apart on exterior lines, para 11 p 132); the frontage row is the plains Bde row, which mountains narrow (GSP 1947 s19 para 12 e p 87).",
